The concrete institutional claim is $\#\{\hbox{Inc. 5000 companies in Miami}\}=139.$ He also points to venture capital support and says Miami ranks as a top-five fastest-growing city in the United States. The Miami case is cumulative rather than single-cause. Its score is not one large term; it is a cluster of reinforcing terms: $S(\text{Miami})
\approx
\hbox{startup density}
+\hbox{collaboration}
+\hbox{institutions}
+\hbox{capital}
+\hbox{city growth}.$ This approximation is not a measured formula. It is a disciplined shorthand for James’s sequence of claims: density brings people together, collaboration helps businesses develop, institutions supply credibility and support, and capital helps firms grow.

